| ARIMA Order (p,d,q) | p | d | q | Model Name   | Description               |
| ------------------- | - | - | - | ------------ | ------------------------- |
| (1,0,0)             | 1 | 0 | 0 | AR(1)        | Auto Regressive Model     |
| (2,0,0)             | 2 | 0 | 0 | AR(2)        | Auto Regressive Model     |
| (0,0,1)             | 0 | 0 | 1 | MA(1)        | Moving Average Model      |
| (0,0,2)             | 0 | 0 | 2 | MA(2)        | Moving Average Model      |
| (1,0,1)             | 1 | 0 | 1 | ARMA(1,1)    | AR + MA Combined          |
| (2,0,1)             | 2 | 0 | 1 | ARMA(2,1)    | AR + MA Combined          |
| (1,1,1)             | 1 | 1 | 1 | ARIMA(1,1,1) | ARMA with Differencing    |
| (2,1,2)             | 2 | 1 | 2 | ARIMA(2,1,2) | ARMA with Differencing    |
| (1,2,1)             | 1 | 2 | 1 | ARIMA(1,2,1) | Second-order Differencing |
| (1,1,1)(1,1,1,12)   | 1 | 1 | 1 | SARIMA       | Seasonal ARIMA            |

==============
| Symbol | Think As        |
| ------ | --------------- |
| **p**  | **Past Values** |
| **d**  | **Difference**  |
| **q**  | **Past Errors** |

===========
p = Auto Regressive (AR) Order

| Parameter | Full Form                 | Purpose                                                                        |
| --------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **p**     | **Auto Regressive Order** | Number of previous observations (lag values) used to predict the current value |

=========

Example

Suppose Britannia stock prices are:

| Day       | Close Price |
| --------- | ----------- |
| Monday    | 6200        |
| Tuesday   | 6250        |
| Wednesday | 6300        |
| Thursday  | ?           |

===========

If p = 1:

Thursday's prediction uses Wednesday's price only.

If p = 2:

Thursday's prediction uses Tuesday's and Wednesday's prices.

If p = 3:

Thursday's prediction uses Monday's, Tuesday's, and Wednesday's prices.
========

Simple Meaning

| p Value | Uses          |
| ------- | ------------- |
| p = 1   | Last 1 value  |
| p = 2   | Last 2 values |
| p = 3   | Last 3 values |

=======

p stands for Auto Regressive Order.
It indicates how many previous observations (lag values) are used by the model to predict the current value. 
For example, p = 2 means the model uses the last two observations for forecasting.

=======

| Parameter | Full Form                  | Meaning                                                   | Example                          |
| --------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **p**     | Auto Regressive (AR) Order | Number of past observations used for prediction           | p = 2 → Uses last 2 days' prices |
| **d**     | Differencing Order         | Number of times data is differenced to make it stationary | d = 1 → First difference         |
| **q**     | Moving Average (MA) Order  | Number of past forecast errors used                       | q = 1 → Uses last 1 error        |

======

| Parameter | Value | Interpretation        |
| --------- | ----- | --------------------- |
| p         | 1     | Uses previous 1 value |
| d         | 1     | Data differenced once |
| q         | 1     | Uses previous 1 error |

======

Stock Price Example

Suppose Britannia closing prices are:

| Day       | Close Price |
| --------- | ----------- |
| Monday    | 6200        |
| Tuesday   | 6250        |
| Wednesday | 6300        |
| Thursday  | 6280        |


p = 1 (AR)

Uses Wednesday's price to predict Thursday.

==============
d = 1

Difference series:

| Day       | Difference        |
| --------- | ----------------- |
| Tuesday   | 6250 - 6200 = 50  |
| Wednesday | 6300 - 6250 = 50  |
| Thursday  | 6280 - 6300 = -20 |


This helps make the series stationary.

========

q = 1 (MA)

Uses the previous forecast error while making the next prediction.

=======

How to Find p, d, q

| Parameter | Method                                   |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| p         | PACF Plot                                |
| d         | ADF Test / Differencing until stationary |
| q         | ACF Plot                                 |

=========

p is the number of lag observations used in the Auto Regressive part, 
d is the number of differencing operations applied to make the series stationary, 
and q is the number of lagged forecast errors used in the Moving Average part. For example,
ARIMA(1,1,1) uses one lag value, one differencing step, and one lag error term.


